. After this there was no regular system of artillery drafts. A draft of 80 was detailed to take charge of horses on board ship as all cavalry drafts were for the time exhausted, and this draft sailed on the 30th June.", "reference": "1904, Great Britain. Parliament. House of Commons, Sessional papers. Inventory control record 1 - Volume 42, page 83:"}, {"text": "The Complaint about the Beef lately furnished for the use of the Troops &c at Parramatta, is not without foundation; when I commenced supplying the several Stations with animal food in January last, — a Draft of very fat Oxen was brought from Wellington for that purpose, — that draft has met the consumption of several Stations until now. — the Cattle lately remaining fell very much off in condition, though I certainly consider they were not inferior to a great deal of Beef, I have seen taken from Contractors.", "reference": "1982, John Maxwell, Brian Edmund Lloyd, Bertha Mac Smith, Letters of John Maxwell, Superintendent of Government Stock, page 206:"}, {"text": "An earlier draft of horses for the 4th Light Dragoons, however,was very different.", "reference": "1993, Lord Anglesey, A History of the British Cavalry 1816-1919: Volume 2: 1851-1871:"}, {"text": "The Light Brigade have got a draft of about 250 horses from England, and we expect ours shortly.", "reference": "2009, Philip Warner, A Cavalryman in the Crimea:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(American spelling, rail transport) The pulling force (tension) on couplers and draft gear during a slack (stretched) condition.", "tags": ["US", "countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The bevel given to the pattern for a casting, so that it can be drawn from the sand without damaging the mould.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The action or an act (especially of a beast of burden or vehicle) of pulling something along or back.", "tags": ["US", "archaic", "countable", "possibly", "uncountable"], "examples": ["using oxen for draft", "shot forth an arrow with a mighty draft"], "quotes": [{"text": "In the late eighteenth and early nineteenth century in the U.S., \"strong\" ploughs requiring the draft of four and sometimes six oxen were frequently used for breaking land which had previously lain fallow for several years.", "reference": "1988, Kenneth W. Russell, Afther Eden, page 39:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The act of drawing in a net for fish.", "tags": ["US", "archaic", "countable", "possibly", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "изваждане"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "vetäminen"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "That which is drawn in; a catch; a haul.", "tags": ["US", "archaic", "countable", "possibly", "uncountable"], "examples": ["He cast his net, which brought him a very great draft."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "улов"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "veto"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "prise"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A small stream or tributary.", "tags": ["Appalachia", "countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["Crossing several ridges & hollows & two small drafts of water."], "quotes": [{"text": "Hollows were tributary to a cove or another type of sizable valley threaded by a creek or, in Pennsylvania, a “run,” and in Virginia, a “draft.”", "reference": "2003, John Alexander Williams, Appalachia: A History, page 113:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations"